  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Tuesday)...\n\nPersistent upper low that has been in the area for the past 5 days\ncontinues to influence the weather over our vicinity. It is \ncurrently centered over southern Saskatchewan and is slowly\ndrifting to the east. Northwest flow wrapping around the low\npushed a weak cold front through the area overnight, with surface\nhigh pressure building over the forecast area at this time.\nSubsident drier air with the high will keep convective potential\nlow today, mainly over the western mountains/foothills, with a \ngood deal of sun this afternoon and temperatures climbing into the\n70s.\n\nSurface high shifts to the east overnight tonight. Return \neasterly flow around the high will tap into plains moisture and \nadvect it into the area in the early morning hours. This moisture \nadvection, aided by a modest low level jet, will be enough to \ngenerate isolated showers over far southeast Montana, possibly \nSheridan County Wyoming, before sunrise tomorrow. This activity \nwill be short lived and will diminish as the atmosphere mixes mid\nto late morning leaving the southeasterly moisture advecting low \nlevel winds in place. By afternoon a progressive trof will arrive \nfrom the west. This feature interacting with the increased \nmoisture and favorable shear profile is expected to generate \nscattered showers and thunderstorms through the afternoon into the\nevening hours. Stronger storms look to get going in the Billings \nvicinity around 1pm and quickly move eastward. Severe potential \nwill be maximized along the axis of strongest moisture advection, \nessentially along the US-212 corridor from Crow Agency eastward \nacross SE Montana. Main thunderstorm threats will be large hail \n(golf ball+), strong wind gusts (65+ mph) and locally heavy \nrainfall. An isolated tornado threat is present, with the highest \nprobabilities from Broadus east across S. Carter County. Keep \nweather aware on Thursday afternoon and have a plan to seek \nshelter if storms approach your location. \n\nRidging builds into the area for Friday for a calmer day \nconvection wise. A deepening trof along the west coast will \nintensify southwest flow aloft through the day resulting in \nincreasing wind gusts over the western mountains and foothills in \nthe afternoon hours. The downslope winds will dry out the lower \natmosphere helping to keep those precipitation/convection chances \nvery low even in the mountains. The increased downslope flow has \nalso boosted temperatures for Friday afternoon compared to \nyesterdays model runs. Latest NBM is showing mid to upper 80s for \nFriday afternoon high temperatures. \n\nSaturday will be a pre-frontal day with downslope winds pushing \ntemperatures toward the lower 90s (a bit cooler than yesterdays \nforecast). A late day cold front will be the focus for generating \nscattered showers and thunderstorms lasting into the overnight \nhours and possibly into Sunday morning across SE Montana. There is\ncertainly enough CAPE/Shear to provide a shot at severe storms \nlate in the day Saturday. Deterministic and Global Ensembles show \nthe best ingredients/potential to reside just to the east of the \nforecast area into the W. Dakotas. However, the ML/AI severe \nthreat guidance is pushing higher potentials well into C. Montana.\nBottom line, if you have outdoor plans for Saturday \nafternoon/evening be weather aware and have a plan to get to a \nsafe location if storms do develop. Still some uncertainty on the \ntiming of the cold front and upper system driving it which will \nhave a big influence on Saturdays thunderstorm potential so stay \ntuned to the forecast.\n\nFrom Sunday afternoon through Tuesday the area is under a Pacific\ntrof pattern with daily chances for showers and afternoon/evening\nthunderstorms. Temperatures will be in the 65 to 75 range for \nSunday, rising toward 80 degrees on Monday, and possibly into the \nmid-80s for Tuesday. Chambers",
